{
  "objection_id": 1,
  "verdict": "holds",
  "reasoning": "The counter addresses resourcing certainty and delivery pace, but not the core failure mechanism: whether the auth layer is safe to expose under self-serve conditions. The webhook management UI analogy is not load-bearing here — webhooks are outbound event delivery; API key self-serve is inbound credential issuance with scope selection, which creates a meaningfully different attack surface and different edge-case profile. A signed SOW confirms two engineers will show up; it does not confirm what they will find when they begin exposing the auth layer to arbitrary enterprise user inputs. The objection was never about whether the team could build for three weeks — it was about whether the auth layer will hold under conditions it has not previously operated in.",
  "would_update_if": "An engineer who has reviewed the auth layer's token lifecycle code — not the product spec, the implementation — confirms in writing that there are no undocumented assumptions, rate-limit gaps, or token revocation edge cases that are currently managed by the solutions team rather than enforced by the system itself. Alternatively, a short spike (2–3 days) scoping the auth layer's self-serve readiness, with findings shared before Q2 commitment is locked.",
  "resolution_summary": "Objection holds: the 3-week estimate has been validated for delivery capacity but not for the self-serve readiness of the underlying auth layer, leaving a material scope risk unaddressed."
}

